artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $X$ be an affine scheme over an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic zero, with coordinate ring $A = k[X]$. Let $\mathfrak{g}$ be a finite-dimensional Lie algebra (the target algebra), and let $\Gamma$ be a finite group acting by automorphisms on both $X$ (hence on $A$) and on $\mathfrak{g}$. Form the Lie algebra $\mathfrak{g} \otimes A$ with the pointwise bracket $[u \otimes a, v \otimes b] = [u,v] \otimes ab$, and let $\Gamma$ act diagonally. The associated equivariant map algebra is
$$M = M(X, \mathfrak{g})^{\Gamma} = (\mathfrak{g} \otimes A)^{\Gamma},$$
the fixed-point subalgebra under this diagonal action.

While the category of finite-dimensional $M$-modules has been studied extensively, develop a representation theory of $M$ beyond the finite-dimensional setting. Specifically, define and investigate natural infinite-dimensional module categories for an arbitrary equivariant map algebra $M$, including:

(a) an appropriate analogue of the Bernstein–Gelfand–Gelfand category $\mathcal{O}$;
(b) a category of integrable highest-weight representations;
(c) a category of quasifinite representations, namely weight modules whose weight spaces are all finite-dimensional;
(d) other suitable infinite-dimensional module categories generalizing those known for loop algebras, current algebras, and affine Kac–Moody algebras.

For each such category, classify the irreducible objects, describe block decompositions, character/extension structure, and identify when classical features (e.g., highest-weight theory, BGG duality, Weyl modules) admit analogues in the general equivariant map algebra setting.
